<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Miloš Obrenović</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1780">1780</a>–<a href="/years/1860">1860</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Miloš Obrenović I · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obrenovic-dynasty">Obrenović dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Miloš Obrenović was the leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-serbian-uprising">Second Serbian Uprising</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, launched at Takovo in 1815. Combining fighting with negotiation, he won recognition as hereditary prince of an autonomous Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, confirmed in 1830. He ruled autocratically until forced to abdicate in 1839, returned in 1858 and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obrenovic-dynasty">Obrenović dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/milos-obrenovic.jpg" alt="Miloš Obrenović" width="320" height="454" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Milo%C5%A1_Obrenovi%C4%87_1848.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Miloš Obrenović was born Miloš Teodorović, the son of Teodor Mihailović from Dobrinja and of Višnja, whose first husband was Obren Martinović. His family descended from the Bratonožići tribe. He fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-serbian-uprising">First Serbian Uprising</a> until it ended in 1813 and was wounded in the battle for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzice">Užice</a>.</p><p>After the death of his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, a voivode of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudnik">Rudnik</a> district, he took the surname Obrenović, derived from the name of his stepfather Obren. When the rising collapsed, he was among the few leaders who stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> to face the returning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, and the Ottoman commander Serčesma left him in charge of his area; Grand Vizier Hurshid Pasha recognised him as governor of the Rudnik nahiya.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Ottoman rule under Sulejman Pasha Skopljak turned harsh, with disarming and extortion, and a rebellion broke out under Hadži-Prodan. In April 1815 Miloš organised and led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-serbian-uprising">Second Serbian Uprising</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> agreed to terms the same year. After the killing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karadorde">Karađorđe Petrović</a> in 1817 he became leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>. Serbia gained some autonomy but stayed under Ottoman sovereignty, with Miloš as absolute ruler.</p><p>A legislative commission he set up between late 1828 and autumn 1830 first set out to translate the Code Napoléon into Serbian; he then invited two legal specialists from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, Vasilije Lazarević and Jovan Hadžić, to prepare civil and criminal codes. In January 1831 he told a great national assembly that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> had ended the direct obligations of Serbian peasants to their former Turkish lords. The Sultan's decrees of 1830 and 1833 made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> a sovereign principality with Miloš as hereditary prince, and a Metropolitanate of Serbia was set up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Miloš was an autocrat who consistently refused to decentralise power, which provoked strong opposition. After one rebellion he agreed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sretenje-constitution">Sretenje Constitution</a> of 1835, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> opposed; he abolished it at the demand of Russia and the Ottomans, and the Constitution of 1838 replaced it.</p><p>He abdicated in 1839 in favour of his sons, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, who died a few weeks later, and Mihailo. Mihailo was deposed in 1842, and the family was out of power until 1858, when Miloš was restored for the last two years of his life. He died on 26 September 1860. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> proclaimed him Father of the Fatherland, and he is also known as Miloš the Great.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Miloš Obrenović” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the collapse of Revolutionary Serbia in 1813, <b>Miloš Obrenović</b> launched the Second Serbian Uprising at Takovo in 1815.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-serbia">Principality of Serbia</a></li><li><blockquote>The Second Serbian Uprising of 1815 under <b>Miloš Obrenović</b> led to the autonomous Principality of Serbia.</blockquote><a href="/state/revolutionary-serbia">Revolutionary Serbia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Gornja Dobrinja, in what is now <a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.95,20.07&amp;year=1832" aria-label="Gornja Dobrinja on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5031 607 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Miloš Obrenović"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5481 877h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5481 877h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.95,20.07&amp;year=1832">What was here in 1832? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
